00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:17.760
as ephemeral deployments gain adoption. Continued exploration of linear time architectures

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:24.440
is following the MRU update and stability experiments. Okay, let's pop the hood on the matrix

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:30.200
recurrent unit update. Everyone talks about linear time sequence models as simple drop-in

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:35.980
replacements for attention, yet the engineering reality involves careful handling of state matrices

00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:42.940
to avoid instability. The matrix recurrent unit works by reshaping token embeddings into an input

00:06:42.940 --> 00:06:49.360
state matrix then cumulatively multiplying those matrices across the sequence so each position

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:56.600
incorporates all prior transformations in a single associative operation. Parallel scan implementations

00:06:56.600 --> 00:07:02.900
exploit this associativity to keep training tractable on GPUs despite the sequential dependency.

00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:09.120
Early experiments showed that naive reshaping produced lost spikes on larger datasets,

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:16.200
switching to LDU factorization with an activation on the diagonal to enforce determinant one

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:21.300
removed the spikes while preserving the model's ability to learn shear transformations

00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:28.360
rather than pure rotations. On the tiny stories task the MRU still trailed a comparable

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:34.840
transformer indicating lower storage capacity remains a core tradeoff even after stability fixes.

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:42.840
Use MRU when cumulative per token computation matters more than raw capacity and you can tolerate

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:49.320
extra engineering to bound the state matrices. The obvious alternative of attention or state space

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:56.720
models wins when you need higher memory bandwidth across long contexts. Tomorrow keep an eye on any

